1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,199
Hi, I am Chaitanya,

2
00:00:01,199 --> 00:00:05,934
and this video is about how the system decides
who can open which textbook.

3
00:00:05,934 --> 00:00:08,964
So I asked for the whole rule in plain
language:

4
00:00:08,964 --> 00:00:12,436
every path a person can take to end up with
permission,

5
00:00:12,436 --> 00:00:15,150
and where that decision actually gets
made.

6
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:18,758
Every time a student clicks a textbook, something
has to decide:

7
00:00:18,758 --> 00:00:20,393
are you allowed to open this?

8
00:00:20,393 --> 00:00:21,393
It sounds simple.

9
00:00:21,393 --> 00:00:22,393
It isn't —

10
00:00:22,393 --> 00:00:26,734
because there are several different ways a person
can end up with permission.

11
00:00:26,734 --> 00:00:28,932
They might have been given it directly.

12
00:00:28,932 --> 00:00:31,751
They might have it because of a class they
joined.

13
00:00:31,751 --> 00:00:34,457
Or they might just be staff, and get
everything.

14
00:00:34,457 --> 00:00:37,050
So the system asks one question, in one
place.

15
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:37,700
Here's what it does.

16
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:42,650
There are three kinds of people here, and the
answer depends on which one you are.

17
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:46,513
If you're an admin — you run the place — you get
every textbook.

18
00:00:46,513 --> 00:00:48,505
No exceptions, nothing to set up.

19
00:00:48,505 --> 00:00:52,851
If you're an instructor, you get every textbook
too, with one exception:

20
00:00:52,851 --> 00:00:54,239
anything marked hidden.

21
00:00:54,239 --> 00:00:58,465
Hidden is the setting for books still being
built, still being tested.

22
00:00:58,465 --> 00:01:00,155
Instructors don't see those.

23
00:01:00,155 --> 00:01:03,535
But notice — nobody has to grant an instructor
anything.

24
00:01:03,535 --> 00:01:06,252
Permission comes automatically with the
role.

25
00:01:06,252 --> 00:01:08,425
If you're a student, it's different.

26
00:01:08,425 --> 00:01:10,779
Students don't get anything by default.

27
00:01:10,779 --> 00:01:14,280
Every book a student can open, someone decided
they could.

28
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:18,162
And there are exactly two ways a student gets
that permission.

29
00:01:18,162 --> 00:01:19,414
The first is direct.

30
00:01:19,414 --> 00:01:21,794
An admin grants them a specific book —

31
00:01:21,794 --> 00:01:26,177
usually because the student asked for it and the
request was approved.

32
00:01:26,177 --> 00:01:28,055
The second is through a class.

33
00:01:28,055 --> 00:01:30,998
An instructor assigns textbooks to their
class,

34
00:01:30,998 --> 00:01:35,005
and every student enrolled in it gets those books
automatically.

35
00:01:35,005 --> 00:01:36,884
Join the class, get the books.

36
00:01:36,884 --> 00:01:38,199
No separate approval.

37
00:01:38,199 --> 00:01:41,642
Those two lists get added together, duplicates
removed.

38
00:01:41,642 --> 00:01:43,208
That's a student's shelf.

39
00:01:43,208 --> 00:01:47,966
One detail worth knowing: if a class gets
archived — say the semester ends —

40
00:01:47,966 --> 00:01:50,534
the books that came with it stop working.

41
00:01:50,534 --> 00:01:54,040
Access came from the class, so it leaves with the
class.

42
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:56,494
Now the part that's actually interesting.

43
00:01:56,494 --> 00:02:00,145
This question gets asked at two completely
different moments.

44
00:02:00,145 --> 00:02:02,778
Once when the student clicks to open a
book.

45
00:02:02,778 --> 00:02:04,274
And again a moment later,

46
00:02:04,274 --> 00:02:09,122
when the textbook itself checks back with the hub
to confirm the person really is

47
00:02:09,122 --> 00:02:10,122
allowed in.

48
00:02:10,122 --> 00:02:13,654
Two different moments, two different pieces of
the system —

49
00:02:13,654 --> 00:02:18,023
asking the same question, in the same place, and
getting the same answer.

50
00:02:18,023 --> 00:02:19,998
That matters more than it sounds.

51
00:02:19,998 --> 00:02:24,187
If those two checks had their own separate logic,
they could disagree.

52
00:02:24,187 --> 00:02:27,240
You'd get a book that opens and then locks you
out.

53
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:30,112
Or worse, one that lets in someone it
shouldn't.

54
00:02:30,112 --> 00:02:34,080
Because the decision lives in exactly one place,
they can't drift apart.

55
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:35,661
So: admins get everything.

56
00:02:35,661 --> 00:02:38,275
Instructors get everything that's
finished.

57
00:02:38,275 --> 00:02:42,713
Students get what they were given, plus whatever
their classes came with.

58
00:02:42,713 --> 00:02:46,786
One question, asked in one place, answered the
same way every time.

59
00:02:46,786 --> 00:02:49,826
And if someone ever adds a new way to get access
—

60
00:02:49,826 --> 00:02:53,899
a whole department buying a licence, say — it
gets added here once,

61
00:02:53,899 --> 00:02:57,060
and every door in the system already knows about
it.

62
00:02:57,060 --> 00:03:00,385
So what does this design get right, and where
does it bite?

63
00:03:00,385 --> 00:03:01,385
Right:

64
00:03:01,385 --> 00:03:06,006
the same question gets asked in two different
places and cannot come back with two

65
00:03:06,006 --> 00:03:07,021
different answers.

66
00:03:07,021 --> 00:03:10,909
Right again: add a new way in, and every door
already knows about it.

67
00:03:10,909 --> 00:03:14,967
Where it bites: access that arrived with a class
leaves with the class —

68
00:03:14,967 --> 00:03:17,630
and nothing tells the student why the book
stopped opening.

69
00:03:17,630 --> 00:03:18,630
Your turn.

70
00:03:18,630 --> 00:03:22,966
Here's the prompt: map every path a user in my
app can take to get permission,

71
00:03:22,966 --> 00:03:26,579
then show me which two paths could ever disagree
with each other.

72
00:03:26,579 --> 00:03:28,246
Run that on your own codebase.

73
00:03:28,246 --> 00:03:32,916
The first half is easy — you'll probably
recognise the answer as soon as you see it.

74
00:03:32,916 --> 00:03:35,306
The second half is the one that finds
bugs,

75
00:03:35,306 --> 00:03:39,920
because two answers can only disagree where the
same question gets answered in more

76
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:40,920
than one place.

77
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:43,700
Pause here, paste it in, and count how many
places yours has.

78
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:44,853
Who can open what.

79
00:03:44,853 --> 00:03:48,440
One question, one place, and the same answer
every time.
